  "aboutAudioTitle": "Audio — sound & PA",
  "aboutAudioSubtitle": "Gain staging, SPL/distance, delays, cardioid subs, cabling.",
  "aboutAudioPageTitle": "Audio — field reference",
  "audioRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "audioRefSection1Title": "Signal chain & gain staging",
  "audioRefSection1FormulasTitle": "Level reference points",
  "audioRefSection1Formula1": "0 dBu ≈ 0.775 V RMS — pro analog reference",
  "audioRefSection1Formula2": "0 dBFS = maximum digital level before clipping — always keep headroom",
  "audioRefSection1Formula3": "Gain staging: set each stage (source → console → amp) for a good signal-to-noise ratio without clipping",
  "audioRefSection1FieldMarksTitle": "Field tips",
  "audioRefSection1FieldMark1": "Aim for an input level pulsing in the green/yellow, never sitting in the red continuously",
  "audioRefSection1FieldMark2": "A poorly gain-staged system sounds noisy even at low volume: the issue is usually a clipped stage upstream",
  "audioRefSection2Title": "SPL & distance",
  "audioRefSection2FormulaTitle": "Inverse square law",
  "audioRefSection2Formula1": "Every doubling of distance = -6 dB SPL in free field",
  "audioRefSection2Formula2": "SPL(d2) = SPL(d1) - 20 × log10(d2 / d1)",
  "audioRefSection2NoteTitle": "Real-world nuance",
  "audioRefSection2Note1": "Indoors, reflections (walls, ceiling) reduce the actual drop-off compared to theory",
  "audioRefSection2Note2": "A well-designed line array falls off more slowly than a point source along the coverage axis",
  "audioRefSection3Title": "Delay alignment (time alignment)",
  "audioRefSection3Paragraph": "A secondary system (delay tower, front fill) must be delayed so its sound arrives at the same time as the main system, perceived as a single source.",
  "audioRefSection3FormulaTitle": "Basic formula",
  "audioRefSection3Formula1": "Delay (ms) = distance between systems (m) / 0.343 m/ms (speed of sound ≈ 343 m/s)",
  "audioRefSection3TipsTitle": "Best practices",
  "audioRefSection3Tip1": "Measure the actual distance between the two sources, not just the distance to the audience",
  "audioRefSection3Tip2": "Add a few extra ms of delay (2-5 ms) to reinforce the precedence effect (Haas effect) and keep the main system as the perceived source",
  "audioRefSection3Tip3": "Check by ear and/or with an analyzer (e.g. phase correlation) after setting",
  "audioRefSection4Title": "Sub directivity (cardioid / end-fire)",
  "audioRefSection4Paragraph": "A cardioid or end-fire arrangement reduces the low-end energy sent toward the stage/FOH while reinforcing it toward the audience, by playing with delay and polarity between subs.",
  "audioRefSection4TypesTitle": "Common configurations",
  "audioRefSection4Type1": "End-fire: subs aligned in a row, the front one advanced and electronically delayed",
  "audioRefSection4Type2": "2-sub cardioid: one front sub (normal), one rear sub polarity-inverted and delayed",
  "audioRefSection4Type3": "3-sub cardioid: two front in phase, one rear inverted and delayed — best level/rear-rejection trade-off",
  "audioRefSection5Title": "Cabling & connectors",
  "audioRefSection5XlrTitle": "Balanced XLR",
  "audioRefSection5XlrBullet1": "Always prefer a balanced link (XLR/TRS) over long distances to reject noise",
  "audioRefSection5XlrBullet2": "Beyond ~50-100 m analog, consider a digital link (AES/EBU, Dante, etc.)",
  "audioRefSection5SpeakonTitle": "Speakon (loudspeakers)",
  "audioRefSection5SpeakonBullet1": "Never use XLR for loudspeaker feeds: the power carried can damage equipment meant for line-level signal",
  "audioRefSection5SpeakonBullet2": "Check the cable gauge is suited to the power and distance (voltage drop) before a long run",
  "audioRefSection5GroundTitle": "Ground & hum",
  "audioRefSection5GroundBullet1": "A ground loop between two devices connected to mains through different paths can create 50/60 Hz hum",
  "audioRefSection5GroundBullet2": "A ground lift or a DI box with galvanic isolation can fix this without cutting the safety earth",
  "audioRefSection6Title": "Hearing safety & best practices",
  "audioRefSection6DontDoTitle": "Avoid",
  "audioRefSection6DontDoBullet1": "Exposing the audience or crew to more than 100 dB SPL continuously with no time limit",
  "audioRefSection6DontDoBullet2": "Pushing a system beyond its rated power to compensate for poor placement",
  "audioRefSection6WarningTitle": "Exposure reference points",
  "audioRefSection6WarningBullet1": "85 dB SPL: recommended limit for prolonged exposure (8h) without protection",
  "audioRefSection6WarningBullet2": "100 dB SPL: only a few minutes before risk — most concerts exceed this level",
  "audioRefSection6WarningBullet3": "Always use a system limiter on the output to protect the gear and the audience",
  "audioRefSection7Title": "Quick checklist",
  "audioRefSection7QuickTitle": "Before the show",
  "audioRefSection7Bullet1": "Gain staging checked from source to amp, no clipping",
  "audioRefSection7Bullet2": "Secondary system delays calculated and applied",
  "audioRefSection7Bullet3": "System limiter active and set",
  "audioRefSection7Bullet4": "Polarity checked between subs and tops (no cancellation)",
  "audioRefSection7Bullet5": "SPL level measured at FOH and at the back of the room",
  "audioRefFooterNote": "These are general reference points — always check the manufacturer's guidelines and local regulations on sound levels.",
  "aboutRfTitle": "RF — wireless & Wi-Fi",
  "aboutRfSubtitle": "UHF bands, Wi-Fi, IM3 coexistence, antennas, RF scanning.",
  "aboutRfPageTitle": "RF — field reference",
  "rfRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "rfRefSection1Title": "RF spectrum basics",
  "rfRefSection1FormulasTitle": "Frequency / wavelength reference",
  "rfRefSection1Formula1": "λ (m) = 300 / f (MHz) — approximate wavelength",
  "rfRefSection1Formula2": "The higher the frequency, the shorter the wavelength and the shorter the free-space range",
  "rfRefSection1FieldMarksTitle": "Field tips",
  "rfRefSection1FieldMark1": "The human body absorbs and reflects RF waves — the position of a wireless receiver on the body affects reception",
  "rfRefSection1FieldMark2": "Always favor a direct line of sight between transmitter and receiver",
  "rfRefSection2Title": "UHF bands for wireless mics",
  "rfRefSection2Paragraph": "Wireless mics and in-ears mainly use the UHF band (470-694 MHz in Europe, depending on the country), shared with TV broadcasting — actual availability depends on local regulations.",
  "rfRefSection2NoteTitle": "Check before every date",
  "rfRefSection2Note1": "Available bands vary by country — check local regulations (ANFR in France, Ofcom in the UK, etc.)",
  "rfRefSection2Note2": "A band free in one place may be occupied by a TV transmitter or another provider elsewhere",
  "rfRefSection3Title": "Wi-Fi 2.4 GHz vs 5 GHz",
  "rfRefSection3TableTitle": "Key differences",
  "rfRefSection3Bullet1": "2.4 GHz: 3 non-overlapping channels (1, 6, 11), better range, more congested (Bluetooth, high-band wireless mics, other networks)",
  "rfRefSection3Bullet2": "5 GHz: many more non-overlapping channels, higher throughput, shorter range, generally less interference",
  "rfRefSection3Bullet3": "For control/monitoring (consoles, visualization), prefer 5 GHz when possible",
  "rfRefSection4Title": "Coexistence & intermodulation (IM3)",
  "rfRefSection4Paragraph": "When several wireless transmitters operate together, their frequencies can create intermodulation products (IM3) that land on other used frequencies and cause interference.",
  "rfRefSection4FormulaTitle": "IM3 formula (3rd order)",
  "rfRefSection4Formula1": "IM3 = 2×f1 − f2 and 2×f2 − f1",
  "rfRefSection4Tip1": "The more wireless channels you add, the higher the risk of an IM3 product landing on a used frequency — hence the value of a frequency plan calculated in advance",
  "rfRefSection5Title": "Antennas & diversity",
  "rfRefSection5Bullet1": "Diversity reception uses two slightly offset antennas to avoid cancellation zones (RF dead spots)",
  "rfRefSection5Bullet2": "Space antennas at least a quarter wavelength apart, without lining them up directly with the transmitter",
  "rfRefSection5Bullet3": "Avoid placing antennas directly on the ground or against a metal structure, which can detune or shield the signal",
  "rfRefSection6Title": "RF scan before a show",
  "rfRefSection6Bullet1": "Always scan the band on site, at show time — the RF environment changes between day and evening (other providers, audience hotspots, etc.)",
  "rfRefSection6Bullet2": "Note the occupied frequencies and document them for the next date at the same venue if possible",
  "rfRefSection6Bullet3": "Keep a margin of free frequencies for a last-minute swap (faulty gear, unexpected interference)",
  "rfRefSection7Title": "Quick checklist",
  "rfRefSection7Bullet1": "RF scan done at show time",
  "rfRefSection7Bullet2": "Frequency plan calculated (IM3 checked)",
  "rfRefSection7Bullet3": "Antennas in line of sight, properly spaced",
  "rfRefSection7Bullet4": "UHF bands used comply with local regulations",
  "rfRefFooterNote": "RF regulations vary widely by country — always check locally authorized frequencies before a show.",
  "aboutLaserTitle": "Laser — physics & safety",
  "aboutLaserSubtitle": "How it works, scanners, classes, standards, NOHD/SZED/CZED.",
  "aboutLaserPageTitle": "Laser — field reference",
  "laserRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "laserRefSection1Title": "What is a laser?",
  "laserRefSection1AcronymTitle": "The LASER acronym",
  "laserRefSection1AcronymBody": "LASER stands for \"Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation\".",
  "laserRefSection1HowTitle": "The physical principle",
  "laserRefSection1HowBody": "A gain medium (diode, DPSS crystal...) is \"pumped\" electrically or optically, triggering stimulated emission of photons. A resonant cavity (two mirrors, one partially reflective) amplifies this light, which comes out coherent, monochromatic and highly collimated — unlike an ordinary light source.",
  "laserRefSection1TypesTitle": "Common types in show lasers",
  "laserRefSection1Type1": "Direct diode: compact, often used in RGB to mix colours",
  "laserRefSection1Type2": "DPSS (Diode Pumped Solid State): a diode pumps a crystal, historically used for green and other specific wavelengths",
  "laserRefSection2Title": "How the beam draws: scanners and speed",
  "laserRefSection2Paragraph": "The beam is steered by two galvanometer mirrors (\"galvos\"), one for the X axis and one for the Y axis, spinning extremely fast. Unlike video (line-by-line raster scan), a laser show draws vector shapes, point by point, tracing the outline of the image.",
  "laserRefSection2SpeedTitle": "Scan speed",
  "laserRefSection2Speed1": "Speed is measured in points per second (pps) or kilopoints per second (kpps)",
  "laserRefSection2Speed2": "ILDA (International Laser Display Association) standardises the connector, the .ild file format, and a test pattern used to objectively benchmark a scanner",
  "laserRefSection2FieldTitle": "Field notes",
  "laserRefSection2Field1": "The more points an image contains, the more pps are needed to avoid flicker — a smooth show typically runs around 20 to 30 kpps",
  "laserRefSection2Field2": "The ILDA test pattern lets you compare two scanners on objective criteria (speed, accuracy, damping)",
  "laserRefSection3Title": "Laser classes & standards",
  "laserRefSection3ClassesTitle": "IEC 60825-1 classes",
  "laserRefSection3Class1": "Class 1: safe under normal use, no special precautions",
  "laserRefSection3Class2": "Class 1M / 2M: safe to the naked eye, but hazardous with optical instruments (binoculars, magnifier)",
  "laserRefSection3Class3": "Class 2: visible, protected by the blink reflex (< 1 mW)",
  "laserRefSection3Class4": "Class 3R: moderate risk, limited direct hazard",
  "laserRefSection3Class5": "Class 3B: hazardous to the eye under direct viewing — most show lasers fall in this class or class 4",
  "laserRefSection3Class6": "Class 4: hazardous to the eye and skin, fire risk — maximum precautions required",
  "laserRefSection3StandardsTitle": "Reference standards",
  "laserRefSection3Standard1": "IEC 60825-1: product safety and laser classification",
  "laserRefSection3Standard2": "IEC 60825-3: specific guidance for laser displays and shows (variable MPE concept depending on exposure duration)",
  "laserRefSection3Standard3": "Local regulations: declaration/authorisation depending on power and country (e.g. FDA/CDRH 21 CFR 1040.10 in the US, with a variance procedure for shows)",
  "laserRefSection3FieldNote": "Always check local regulations before a show, especially for audience scanning and near an airport.",
  "laserRefSection4Title": "Safety measures",
  "laserRefSection4Bullet1": "A trained Laser Safety Officer (LSO) is designated and responsible for the show",
  "laserRefSection4Bullet2": "Key switch and emergency stop are easily accessible",
  "laserRefSection4Bullet3": "An interlock cuts emission if a cover or housing is opened",
  "laserRefSection4Bullet4": "Scan-fail detection cuts the beam if the galvos stop moving — essential for audience scanning",
  "laserRefSection4Bullet5": "No static beam pointed at the audience — only audience scanning with dedicated protections and exposure calculations",
  "laserRefSection4Bullet6": "Never aim at airspace, an aircraft, or an uncontrolled reflective surface (glass, mirror, screen)",
  "laserRefSection5Title": "NOHD, SZED, CZED explained",
  "laserRefSection5Paragraph": "These safety distances are calculated by the app's \"Safety zones\" tool from the beam's power, divergence and output diameter.",
  "laserRefSection5NohdTitle": "NOHD — Nominal Ocular Hazard Distance",
  "laserRefSection5NohdBody": "Nominal ocular hazard distance: beyond this distance, the beam's direct irradiance drops below the maximum permissible exposure (MPE) for a brief, accidental exposure. This is the baseline reference defined by IEC 60825-1.",
  "laserRefSection5SzedTitle": "SZED — Show Zone Exposure Distance",
  "laserRefSection5SzedBody": "Show zone exposure distance: marks the zone where the audience may be positioned during a show involving beam scanning (audience scanning), using a more cautious MPE threshold than the NOHD.",
  "laserRefSection5CzedTitle": "CZED — Controlled Zone Exposure Distance",
  "laserRefSection5CzedBody": "Controlled zone exposure distance: the most cautious of the three, reserved for the zone where only trained personnel (operators, technicians) may stand, accounting for potentially longer exposure.",
  "laserRefSection5FieldNote": "In the app, the lower the MPE threshold used, the larger the calculated distance — hence the order CZED > SZED > NOHD. These distances remain indicative: they don't replace an assessment by a qualified Laser Safety Officer or applicable local regulations.",
  "laserRefSection6Title": "Quick checklist",
  "laserRefSection6Bullet1": "Laser class identified, declared if required",
  "laserRefSection6Bullet2": "LSO designated and present throughout the show",
  "laserRefSection6Bullet3": "NOHD/SZED/CZED zones calculated and marked out",
  "laserRefSection6Bullet4": "Scan-fail detection active if audience scanning",
  "laserRefSection6Bullet5": "Emergency stop tested and accessible",
  "laserRefSection6Bullet6": "No aiming at airspace or an uncontrolled reflective surface",
  "laserRefFooterNote": "This information is general and educational — it does not replace Laser Safety Officer training or local regulations (IEC 60825, FDA/CDRH, aviation authorities...).",
  "aboutRiggingTitle": "Rigging",
  "aboutRiggingSubtitle": "Basics, bridles and angle factor",
  "aboutRiggingPageTitle": "Rigging — quick course",
  "riggingRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "riggingRefSection1Title": "Rigging basics",
  "riggingRefSection2Title": "2-point bridle rigging",
  "riggingRefSection3Title": "Angle factor",
  "riggingRefSection4Title": "Checklist",
  "riggingRefSection1Bullet1": "WLL (Working Load Limit): the maximum rated load for an accessory, as specified by the manufacturer.",
  "riggingRefSection1Bullet2": "Safety factor: the ratio between breaking load and WLL, typically 5:1 or 10:1 depending on the equipment and regulations.",
  "riggingRefSection1Bullet3": "Load chain: every link (hang point, sling, shackle, structure) must be rated for the full load — the weakest link sets the limit for the whole chain.",
  "riggingRefSection2Bullet1": "A 2-point bridle splits the load between two slings forming an angle relative to vertical.",
  "riggingRefSection2Bullet2": "The larger the angle from vertical, the higher the tension in each sling.",
  "riggingRefSection2Bullet3": "Common recommendation: keep the included angle between the two legs under 90° to limit added tension.",
  "riggingRefSection3FormulaTitle": "Formula",
  "riggingRefSection3Formula1": "Tension per sling = (Load / number of slings) × angle factor, where angle factor = 1 / cos(angle from vertical).",
  "riggingRefSection3Bullet1": "Examples: 0° → factor 1.0; 45° → factor about 1.41; 60° → factor 2.0.",
  "riggingRefSection3Bullet2": "The larger the angle from vertical, the faster the factor — and therefore the tension — increases.",
  "riggingRefSection4Bullet1": "Check the WLL of every accessory (sling, shackle, carabiner, structure) before rigging.",
  "riggingRefSection4Bullet2": "Calculate the actual tension per sling using the angle factor, not just the total load divided by the number of legs.",
  "riggingRefSection4Bullet3": "Visually inspect accessories (deformation, corrosion, wear) before every use.",
  "riggingRefSection4Bullet4": "Have the setup checked by a competent person or qualified rigger for heavy loads or complex configurations.",
  "riggingRefFooterNote": "This content is a general educational reminder and does not replace local standards, rigger training, or verification by a competent person.",
  "aboutConsoleTitle": "Console & patch",
  "aboutConsoleSubtitle": "Virtual console, GDTF and DMX patch",
  "aboutConsolePageTitle": "Console — quick course",
  "consoleRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "consoleRefSection1Title": "Virtual console & wireless DMX",
  "consoleRefSection2Title": "GDTF & GDTF Share",
  "consoleRefSection3Title": "Patch & universes",
  "consoleRefSection4Title": "Checklist",
  "consoleRefSection1Paragraph": "A virtual console lets you run a show from a tablet or smartphone, sending DMX over the network (Art-Net or sACN) instead of a direct DMX cable.",
  "consoleRefSection1Bullet1": "Wi-Fi adds latency and dropout risk: reserve it for setup work, not for critical live cues without a wired fallback.",
  "consoleRefSection1Bullet2": "Make sure the DMX-over-IP node/interface actually receives the correct Art-Net or sACN universe configured on the virtual console.",
  "consoleRefSection1Bullet3": "Keep a wired backup (direct DMX or Ethernet) ready for critical moments of the show.",
  "consoleRefSection2GdtfTitle": "GDTF (General Device Type Format)",
  "consoleRefSection2GdtfBody": "A standardized file format describing a fixture's characteristics (DMX modes, color wheels, 3D geometry) for reliable import into any compatible console.",
  "consoleRefSection2ShareTitle": "GDTF Share",
  "consoleRefSection2ShareBody": "The official online database where manufacturers publish GDTF files for their fixtures, free to download for patching.",
  "consoleRefSection3Bullet1": "A DMX universe holds 512 channels; each fixture uses one or more channels depending on its selected DMX mode.",
  "consoleRefSection3Bullet2": "Always check the DMX mode actually set on the physical fixture — it must match the one selected in the console's patch.",
  "consoleRefSection3Bullet3": "Documenting the patch (addresses, universes, modes) makes troubleshooting and handover to another operator much easier.",
  "consoleRefSection4Bullet1": "Check the network connection (Wi-Fi or wired) between the virtual console and the DMX node before the show.",
  "consoleRefSection4Bullet2": "Confirm every fixture is patched to the right universe and start address.",
  "consoleRefSection4Bullet3": "Use an up-to-date GDTF file from GDTF Share rather than a generic profile.",
  "consoleRefSection4Bullet4": "Plan a fallback in case of network or Wi-Fi dropout during the show.",
  "consoleRefFooterNote": "This content is a general reminder; refer to the manufacturer's documentation for specific DMX modes and procedures.",
  "aboutCinemaTitle": "Cinema",
  "aboutCinemaSubtitle": "Camera, depth of field and frame rate",
  "aboutCinemaPageTitle": "Cinema — quick course",
  "cinemaRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "cinemaRefSection1Title": "Camera basics",
  "cinemaRefSection2Title": "Depth of field & focus",
  "cinemaRefSection3Title": "Frame rate & shutter",
  "cinemaRefSection4Title": "Checklist",
  "cinemaRefSection1Bullet1": "Sensor: the light-sensitive surface that captures the image; its size affects field of view and depth of field for a given focal length.",
  "cinemaRefSection1Bullet2": "Focal length: the distance (in mm) that determines the angle of view — a short focal length is wide-angle, a long one is telephoto.",
  "cinemaRefSection1Bullet3": "Aperture (f/): the size of the diaphragm opening, controlling both the amount of light entering and the depth of field.",
  "cinemaRefSection2Paragraph": "Depth of field is the zone of acceptable sharpness in front of and behind the subject in focus.",
  "cinemaRefSection2Bullet1": "A wide aperture (f/1.4, f/2) gives shallow depth of field (blurred background).",
  "cinemaRefSection2Bullet2": "A narrow aperture (f/11, f/16) gives extended depth of field (everything in focus).",
  "cinemaRefSection3FormulaTitle": "Shutter angle formula",
  "cinemaRefSection3Formula1": "Shutter speed = 1 / (2 × frame rate in fps) for a 180° shutter angle, the standard reference in filmmaking.",
  "cinemaRefSection3Bullet1": "24 fps is the classic cinema frame rate; 25 or 30 fps are common in broadcast depending on the video standard (PAL/NTSC).",
  "cinemaRefSection3Bullet2": "A narrower shutter angle (90°, 45°) gives a more staccato look, often used for stylized action scenes.",
  "cinemaRefSection4Bullet1": "Check that the camera frame rate matches the frequency of light sources (to avoid flicker).",
  "cinemaRefSection4Bullet2": "Choose aperture based on the desired depth-of-field look, not just exposure.",
  "cinemaRefSection4Bullet3": "Keep a consistent shutter angle throughout the shoot for a uniform motion texture.",
  "cinemaRefSection4Bullet4": "Always test under real set conditions before the final shoot.",
  "cinemaRefFooterNote": "This content is a general educational reminder; final settings depend on the equipment and creative intent.",
  "aboutTimecodeTitle": "Timecode",
  "aboutTimecodeSubtitle": "SMPTE, drop-frame and frame rates",
  "aboutTimecodePageTitle": "Timecode — quick course",
  "timecodeRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "timecodeRefSection1Title": "What is SMPTE timecode",
  "timecodeRefSection2Title": "Drop-frame vs non-drop-frame",
  "timecodeRefSection3Title": "Common frame rates",
  "timecodeRefSection4Title": "Checklist",
  "timecodeRefSection1FormatTitle": "Format",
  "timecodeRefSection1FormatBody": "HH:MM:SS:FF — hours, minutes, seconds and frame number, resetting each time a full second's worth of frames has elapsed.",
  "timecodeRefSection1Bullet1": "SMPTE timecode synchronizes picture, sound and lighting to a shared time reference.",
  "timecodeRefSection1Bullet2": "Each frame carries a unique address, enabling precise referencing in post-production as well as live show control.",
  "timecodeRefSection2Paragraph": "Drop-frame (DF) exists because some frame rates (29.97 fps) are not a whole number of frames per second, which causes the timecode to drift from real time if left uncorrected.",
  "timecodeRefSection2Bullet1": "Non-drop-frame (NDF): counts frames without skipping; simple, but drifts slightly from real time at 29.97 fps.",
  "timecodeRefSection2Bullet2": "Drop-frame (DF): periodically skips frame numbers (not actual frames) to realign the timecode with real time.",
  "timecodeRefSection3Bullet1": "24 fps: the classic cinema frame rate.",
  "timecodeRefSection3Bullet2": "25 fps: the PAL standard (Europe); 30 fps (or 29.97 DF/NDF): the NTSC standard (North America, Japan).",
  "timecodeRefSection3Bullet3": "50 fps and 59.94/60 fps: high frame rates used in broadcast and for slow motion.",
  "timecodeRefSection3Bullet4": "Always check that all sources (camera, sound, lighting, video) use the same reference frame rate.",
  "timecodeRefSection4Bullet1": "Confirm the frame rate and mode (DF/NDF) on all sources before the show or shoot begins.",
  "timecodeRefSection4Bullet2": "Make sure the master timecode source (master clock) is clearly identified and distributed to all devices.",
  "timecodeRefSection4Bullet3": "If drift is suspected, compare the displayed timecode against a reference clock over a long enough period.",
  "timecodeRefFooterNote": "This content is a general reminder; refer to the SMPTE specifications and the documentation of the devices in use.",
  "aboutConversionsTitle": "Conversions & BPM",
  "aboutConversionsSubtitle": "Units, BPM and DMX at a glance",
  "aboutConversionsPageTitle": "Conversions & BPM — quick course",
  "conversionsRefTocCopyText": "Copy summary",
  "conversionsRefSection1Title": "Why convert",
  "conversionsRefSection2Title": "BPM ↔ milliseconds",
  "conversionsRefSection3Title": "DMX ↔ percentage / 8-bit ↔ 16-bit",
  "conversionsRefSection4Title": "Checklist",
  "conversionsRefSection1Paragraph": "Units often get mixed up at FOH (musical BPM, delay milliseconds, DMX percentages, 8/16-bit values): converting quickly and accurately avoids mismatches between lighting, sound and video cues.",
  "conversionsRefSection2FormulaTitle": "Formula",
  "conversionsRefSection2Formula1": "Beat duration (ms) = 60,000 / BPM.",
  "conversionsRefSection2Bullet1": "Example: at 120 BPM, a beat lasts 500 ms; a sixteenth note (1/4 beat) lasts 125 ms.",
  "conversionsRefSection2Bullet2": "Useful for syncing a lighting effect, an audio delay or a video clip to a track's tempo.",
  "conversionsRefSection3Bullet1": "An 8-bit DMX value ranges from 0 to 255; as a percentage, 255 corresponds to 100%.",
  "conversionsRefSection3Bullet2": "In 16-bit, two combined channels (fine/coarse) give a resolution from 0 to 65,535 for finer control (dimmer, pan/tilt).",
  "conversionsRefSection3Bullet3": "Check the mode (8-bit or 16-bit) actually configured on the fixture before patching, otherwise values will be mismatched.",
  "conversionsRefSection4Bullet1": "Always check the displayed unit before entering a value (ms, BPM, %, 8-bit, 16-bit).",
  "conversionsRefSection4Bullet2": "Recalculate rather than round from memory for critical values (sync, safety).",
  "conversionsRefSection4Bullet3": "Document the conversions used in the show's tech rider to make handover easier.",
  "conversionsRefFooterNote": "This content is a general reminder; refer to the device's documentation for its exact resolution modes.",
